# Target C: loaded cap tips and exact paired-packet gluing

**Date:** 2026-10-06. **Authority:**
`a2811924d18778e8220c42904c9a5a92b58ffd78`,
state `2026-10-06.audit.66`. **Worker:** 2, actual exterior caps.
**Classification:** proved candidates awaiting independent audit for the
finite geometric statements; conditional for their cap-closure application;
regression for the sharp finite examples. Default lifecycle: unreviewed.
C-II, the exterior-cap branch, and Level I remain open.

The implication being attacked is

```text
two actual macroscopic antipodal caps
 -> four separated actual loaded endpoints
 -> delete those diameter-carrying endpoints and both full cell populations
 -> protected packets glue with every cross lower pair checked
 -> exact total cardinality and contact/diameter ledger
 -> contradiction if the required packet population is supplied.
```

The packet population is not supplied here. The new implications remove
the old restriction that a deletable wall vertex be unloaded, and remove
an independent cross-packet compatibility hypothesis for the stated
paired regions. They do not infer net insertion credit from safe area.

## 1. Dependencies and provenance

Canonical inputs are the full-source normalization, actual diameter
endpoints being extreme vertices of K, the G-x loaded-component property,
positive unloaded equilibrium, convex contact/pocket geometry, and
`C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT` from audit.64.
The unit-edge crossing identity is also canonical.

Audit.66 now promotes the collective **closed unit wall** shield from
Worker 1 at `e6d04c4d`. Only its precise elementary implication is used:
separated deleted guards let a point be inside at most one deleted unit
disk, after which one local vertex-cone argument suffices. A cap closing
chord is still nonunit. The new one-unit-side hull-tip argument below is
not covered by that promotion.

The actual convex cells and their assigned isolate populations are the
proved candidates M.1--M.2 of
[the partial-wall report](target_c_exterior_cap_mixed_boundary_wall_capacity_2026-10-06.md).
The only proof-only asymptotic input is the previously audited implication
from [antipodal cap pairing](target_c_antipodal_exterior_cap_pairing_2026-09-25.md):
the two cap limits, including their endpoints, are antipodal. Inter-cap
error is o(D); no exact symmetry or O(sqrt(D)) inter-cap error is assumed.
The finite statements below do not require antipodality.

Worker 1's newer curved closed-wall packets at `1ca17812`, `91b61011`, and
`d7395e79` were read. Their rounded population bound uses a complete unit
wall with four side families. Q has a nonunit source-hull arc. Neither that
population bound nor its packet is imported into Q. In particular, the
discrete Green bow inequality does not give an exterior population bound
or an actual cap lattice chart.

## 2. Each cap cell has one hull arc and one unit path

Use G, B, F, K, and Gamma=G union partial(K) as in M.1. Core vertices on
partial(K) are exactly B. Between consecutive loaded vertices a,b, the
source-hull arc has no internal core vertex. Its internal source vertices
are isolates and have degree two in Gamma. If this arc is already a core
edge, it is one unit hull contact; otherwise adding the entire arc to the
connected G increases E-V by one. Let r be the number of arcs that are
not already core edges.

**Lemma T.0.** Each new bounded face of Gamma contains exactly one of
these r added arcs. In particular, the cell Q containing an actual
occupied cap between a,b is bounded by that full source-hull arc and a
simple unit-contact path P from b to a. Its two hull-arc tips a,b are the
actual loaded endpoints of the cap base.

**Proof.** Euler gives exactly r new bounded faces; the old bounded G
faces are unchanged. A new face must contain an added edge, since a face
with its entire boundary and no interior edge in G was already a G face.
Every added arc belongs on its K-interior side to just one face: each
internal arc vertex has degree two and no core edge intervenes. Thus r
arcs are distributed over r new faces, with at least one per face.
There is exactly one per face. Gamma is 2-connected by M.1, so the rest
of each face boundary is a simple path, consisting of unit G edges. QED.

Write W_Q for the vertices of P and

    S_Q=(A intersect Q) minus W_Q.

These are all contact isolates, including every source point on the
pure source-hull arc. At either tip there is one incident unit path side
and one pure source-hull side. Any other vertex of P has two incident
unit path sides, including any additional loaded vertex on P. No claim
that such additional loaded vertices are absent is needed.

## 3. Deleting a loaded hull tip needs only one unit guard

Let v be a hull-arc tip of Q, w its unit-path neighbor, and h the ray of
the pure hull side at v. Put v=0, w=(1,0), and orient the plane so that
the Q vertex cone is

    0<arg(x)<theta,    h has angle theta,    0<theta<pi.

The hull-side supporting half-plane containing K consists of directions
in [theta-pi,theta]. This is the extra exterior premise unavailable at an
arbitrary opened internal wall.

**Lemma T.1 (one-unit-side tip shield).** If x is in int(Q), unit-clear
of every other path vertex, and no guard is imposed at v, then x is
unit-clear of every old source point outside Q other than v. Strict path
guards give strict clearance. With nonstrict guards, every old nonwall
point outside Q is still strictly clear; possible new-old unit contacts
are confined to the explicitly guarded path vertices.

**Proof.** If x is at least one from v, it is at least one from all
original wall vertices. The first exit of xq cannot be through a pure
hull side: x is in int(K), q is in K, and the segment is in int(K)
before q. At a unit wall cd, the weighted-square identity bounds each
segment piece below by sqrt(3)/2, giving |x-q|>=sqrt(3)>1. A vertex exit
gives at least two.

Otherwise set r=|x|<1. The guard at w implies

    x dot w <= r^2/2 < 1/2.                              (1)

An old q outside the Q cone but in K has argument in [theta-pi,0].
If x has argument phi in (0,theta), its scalar product with a unit
vector in that interval is maximized uniquely at the unit ray w:
phi-arg(q) is in [phi,phi+pi-theta], contained in (0,pi).
Consequently x dot q <= |q|(x dot w). Original source separation
gives |q|>=1, so

    |x-q|^2 >= r^2+1-2(x dot w) >= 1.                  (2)

Equality requires q=w, already handled by its guard. For a q inside
the cone but outside Q, its segment cannot exit through either incident
ray or v. It cannot exit early through the source hull either. Its
first exit is a different unit path side, whose two vertices are guarded;
the crossing bound applies. This also proves the claimed strictness
for nonwall points with nonstrict guards. QED.

The same proof covers r=1 by the first case. Notice that q need not be
an old source point for the geometric argument: it is enough that q
is in K, outside Q, and unit-clear of the original path vertices and v.
This form will check new-new pairs below.

**Lemma T.2 (acute loaded tips).** In a canonical source, each of Q's
two loaded tip angles is strictly below pi/2.

**Proof.** Suppose theta>pi/2. Choose a ray strictly inside the Q cone
at angle phi in (pi/2,theta); a point x tending to v on it is strictly
farther than one from w. At theta=pi/2 use

    x=(t^2/4,t),   t>0 sufficiently small.

It is strictly inside Q and

    |x-w|^2=1+t^2/2+t^4/16>1.

Every other path vertex is strictly unit-clear of v. A nonconsecutive
unit edge from v to a path vertex would cross the convex face interior
and split Q, so the only path unit neighbor of v is w. Finiteness
therefore preserves all its other path guards for small t. Every S_Q
point is a contact isolate, so its strict distance from v also persists.
T.1 checks all remaining outside source points. Replace v alone by x.
No contact is created, at least vw is removed, and triangle-freeness is
preserved. All new upper pairs are strict because x is in int(K).
In addition v is an actual diameter endpoint. The canonical audit.64
endpoint with j=0<t gives a contradiction. QED.

Let T_P be the sum of internal left turns on the oriented unit path
and T_K the sum of the internal turns on the pure hull arc. Convexity
and total polygon turn give the useful exact consequence

    T_P+T_K=theta_a+theta_b<pi,   hence T_P<pi.         (3)

This is an exterior unit **arc**, rather than a closed unit wall.

## 4. A finite displacement gap at each deleted loaded tip

For x in int(Q) with |x-w|>=1, its argument phi is strictly between
0 and theta_v. Since theta_v<pi/2,

    |x-v|^2 >= 2(x-v) dot (w-v)
             > 2|x-v| cos(theta_v).

Thus every proposed interior site, even one with exact contacts, satisfies

    |x-v| > 2 cos(theta_v) > 0.                         (4)

This explains why deleting the loaded endpoint cannot be implemented by
an arbitrarily small movement into its own cap cell. It is a finite
source-dependent gap; there is no claimed uniform gap across sources.
For theta_v<=pi/3, the missing tip guard is redundant: (4) already gives
|x-v|>1. Deleting that tip alone does not enlarge this protected region.

The exact rational cone of angle arctan(4/3) has cos(theta)=3/5 and
gap 6/5. This does not refute a nonlocal replacement or a motion of the
retained core. It rules out treating the old loaded site as an interior
site by continuity.

**Lemma T.2a (exact full-guard population).** Put

    W_{Q,empty}={x in int(Q): |x-w|>1 for every w in W_Q}.

Its unit-separated capacity in a canonical source is exactly |S_Q|.

**Proof.** The old S_Q points have every lower distance strictly above
one. A sufficiently small common contraction about an interior Q point
moves every outer-boundary isolate into int(Q), while preserving every
finite strict mutual and wall margin. This supplies |S_Q| sites.

Conversely, if |S_Q|+1 sites fit, delete S_Q and any contact vertex of
the unit path. Keep the strict guard at that deleted vertex as well.
Tiny packet dilation opens all mutual contacts. The all-guard unit-edge
exit identity shields every retained outside source point; every new
upper pair is strict by interiority in K. The new source is triangle-free,
has exactly the old cardinality, and loses at least one old contact.
Audit.64 gives a contradiction. QED.

This is an exact source count, not an estimate from area. In particular,
when a loaded tip angle is at most pi/3, deleting that tip adds **zero**
strict packet capacity: its missing guard is redundant by (4), and the
region still has exactly |S_Q| sites. If both tips have that angle bound,
their joint deletion likewise adds zero strict capacity. Two additional
points cannot be inferred from the two missing labels.

More generally, a deleted internal unit-path vertex of angle psi<=2pi/3
also has a redundant guard in the strict region: the two strict neighbor
guards give |x-v|>2cos(psi/2)>=1. Its neighbors remain guarded whenever
deleted vertices are pairwise at least four apart. Thus, if every bank
vertex on Q has its appropriate angle bound (pi/3 at a hull tip and
2pi/3 at an internal path vertex), the collectively relaxed **strict**
region equals W_{Q,empty} exactly.

This excludes the proposed fixed-wall strict endpoint-refill mechanism
in that angle regime. It does not exclude the source or provide an
exact-contact or moved-core replacement. In particular it cannot be
advertised as exterior-cap elimination.

## 5. The four actual endpoints form a separated bank

Let C_1,C_2 be the actual antipodal macroscopic cap pair. The proof-only
pairing theorem gives opposite normalized disk-cap limits. If theta is
their limiting half-width, macroscopic area gives theta>0. Canonical
centered inradius of F is (sin(rho)/2-o(1))D, where rho=cos(rho), so

    theta <= pi/2-rho < pi/2.

The four normalized endpoints consequently have angular separations
bounded below by min(2theta,pi-2theta)>0 and norm tending to 1/2.
Every subsequential limit has four distinct endpoints. Compactness for
a fixed positive cap-area lower bound supplies a constant gamma>0 with

    min_{v!=w in R}|v-w| >= gamma D,  eventually,       (5)

where R consists of the two actual loaded tips of each cell. In
particular R has four points and satisfies pair separation at least four
for sufficiently large sources. No exact central symmetry is used.

## 6. Collective shield in each actual cell

For the four-point bank R above define

    N_i={x in int(Q_i): |x-w|>=1 for w in W_i minus R},
    W_i^o={x in int(Q_i): |x-w|>1 for w in W_i minus R}.

The symbol W_i^o is a region; W_i is the original unit-path vertex set.
Other-cap endpoints that happen to lie on this path are also deleted
guards. No source point in S_i is retained in the eventual exchange.

**Lemma T.3.** Every x in N_i is unit-clear of every original point in
A minus (S_i union R). It is strictly clear of every such point not
on W_i. With the strict region W_i^o, all these distances are strict.
Moreover x is strictly clear of any original member of R not on W_i.

**Proof.** Pair separation of R implies that x is within distance
strictly below one of at most one deleted guard. If it is not, all
original wall distances are at least one, and the unit-edge crossing
bound proves the result. If it is close to v in R intersect W_i, all
other deleted guards are at distance at least three. Its incident unit
neighbors are retained. A hull tip has the one-unit-side shield T.1;
an internal path vertex has the two-unit-ray cone argument of M.2 and
canonical audit.63/66. The first exit cannot use a pure hull side.
All other exit vertices have guards at least one. This proves strict
clearance of every nonwall point, including original R points outside
the wall, by exactly the same source-separation inequalities. At exact
distance one from a deleted guard the first crossing case applies. QED.

## 7. Automatic cross-packet separation

**Theorem T.4.** If Q_1,Q_2 are distinct actual cells with distinct
hull-arc tips, R is their four tips, and pair distances in R are at
least four, then

    |x-y|>1 for every x in N_1 and y in N_2.             (6)

The result includes nonstrict wall guards. Thus every pair of locally
unit-separated packets in these regions is globally unit-separated,
and it creates no cross-packet contacts or triangles.

**Proof.** Call a point close to R if its distance to some v in R is
at most one. There is at most one such v. By T.3 a close deleted point
must be on that point's own path boundary; old deleted points outside
the path are strictly clear.

If x and y are close to different v,w, then

    |x-y| >= |v-w|-|x-v|-|y-w| >= 2>1.

They cannot both be close to the same v. Here v is an extreme point of
K, so distinct incident face sectors have total angle strictly below pi.
An internal unit-path sector of angle psi admits a point at radius r<=1
guarding both unit neighbors only if

    r>=2 cos(psi/2),  hence psi>=2pi/3.                 (7)

A hull-tip sector admits a point at radius r<=1 guarding its one unit
neighbor only if its angle is strictly above pi/3, by (4). The same v
cannot be a hull tip of both cells because their four tips are distinct.
Two internal sectors would require total angle at least 4pi/3; an
internal sector and a tip sector would require total angle greater than
pi. Both contradict the hull cone bound.

It remains that neither point is close to R, or just one is. T.3 checks
every original retained wall vertex of the other cell; a point far from
R is also unit-clear of every deleted vertex. If both points are far,
follow xy to the first unit-wall exit of Q_1: both endpoints guard that
wall, so |x-y|>=sqrt(3)>1 (or at least two for a vertex exit).

If only x is close to v, y guards every original Q_1 wall vertex and v.
Use the geometric T.1 or two-unit-cone proof for x against y instead of
an old q. If y is outside the local cone, equality in the cone estimate
could occur only at an original unit wall neighbor. But y is in int(Q_2),
and an original core wall point is never inside that face. If y is in
the cone, its first exit from Q_1 is a different guarded unit wall.
Thus |x-y|>1. Exchange the two cells for the remaining case. QED.

This is not an independent-cap area sum. It is a finite geometric proof
of every new cross lower distance, including the deleted-vertex corners.
The distinct-tip hypothesis is essential; Section 10 preserves its
failure when two cells share an actual loaded tip.

## 8. Exact paired-cap diameter-endpoint exchange

The populations S_1,S_2 are disjoint: face interiors are disjoint and
their only shared boundary source points are core wall vertices. A hull
isolate is assigned to just one pure arc. Define

    R_all=S_1 union S_2 union R,
    m=|S_1|+|S_2|+4.

Every R point is an actual diameter endpoint and a contact vertex. Let
t be the number of original contacts lost and d the number of original
actual diameter pairs lost, with edges counted once. Since R vertices
are pairwise at least four apart, no old contact joins two of them, and

    t=sum_{v in R} deg_C(v),    4<=t<=20,    d>0.        (8)

The upper bound uses triangle-freeness: six unit neighbors would force
six angular gaps exactly 60 degrees and hence a unit triangle.

**Theorem T.5 (conditional paired loaded-core release).** Suppose finite
packets Y_i subset N_i are locally unit-separated and their total size
is m. Count every packet-internal and packet-to-retained-wall unit pair
as j_i. If each complete local new contact graph is triangle-free and

    j_1+j_2<=t,                                        (9)

the canonical source is impossible.

**Proof.** Delete R_all and insert Y_1 union Y_2. T.3 checks every
new-old lower pair; T.4 checks every cross new pair strictly. Every
inserted point is in int(K), so every involving upper pair is <D.
New unit contacts are exactly the j_1+j_2 pairs. The full new graph is
triangle-free: retained old triangles do not exist, and a triangle
meeting both packets would require a forbidden cross contact. The
local triangle check includes both retained anchors of any triangle.
Cardinality is m on both sides. All old diameter pairs meeting R are
removed, and none are created. Audit.64 applies with j<=t and d>0,
including the case j=t. QED.

If R_all also covers all old diameter pairs, strict diameter follows
without any contact or triangle restriction. That coverage is not
claimed for these four endpoints alone.

**Strict-capacity corollary.** Let M_i=Pack_1(W_i^o). Then

    M_1+M_2 >= |S_1|+|S_2|+4                            (10)

would contradict the canonical source. Select exactly m sites and dilate
each finite packet slightly: all internal unit pairs open, the strict
wall guards persist, and T.4 still supplies strict cross separation.
Then j=0<t. Thus an actual surviving pair necessarily satisfies

    M_1+M_2 <= |S_1|+|S_2|+3.                           (11)

Every M_i is at least |S_i|: each old S_i point is strict-clear of every
old source point, and its finitely many boundary points can be moved
slightly into Q_i with all strict pair margins preserved. No packet
needs to be allocated exactly |S_i|+2 sites separately; (10) is a total
cardinality comparison with proved cross compatibility.

For a paid exact-contact packet, at most 2j<=40 inserted vertices are
incident with new contacts. Its quadratic remaining population is strict.
This localizes a contact ledger; it does not supply the m sites.

## 9. One site cannot be pinned by a half-turn unit arc

Here all internal vertices of a convex unit path P are guarded. Only
its endpoints may be missing guards. Let its internal turn be <pi.
Let x be in the Q side of P, unit-clear of all retained path vertices,
and suppose the contact graph formed by x and the path is triangle-free.

**Lemma T.6.** The unit-neighbor vectors from x to retained path vertices
do not strictly surround the origin. Consequently all these contacts
can be opened by an arbitrarily small finite movement of x, by the
canonical audit.64 one-site argument.

**Proof.** If they strictly surrounded x, the polar gaps between cyclic
unit neighbors would all be <pi. Choose three consecutive such neighbors
a,b,c in path order. Write their unwrapped polar angles about x as
phi_a<phi_b<phi_c, with both intervening gaps <pi. Orient P with Q
on its left. Its edge directions are monotone and x is left of every
path edge.

Let p be the path vertex immediately after a. It is not b: otherwise
xa,xb,ab form a unit triangle. It is an internal path vertex between
a and c and therefore is guarded. Put x=0, |a|=|b|=1, and let eta
be the direction of the unit vector p-a. Guarding p and left-sidedness
give 0<eta-phi_a<=2pi/3. Original wall separation |p-b|>=1 gives

    (b-a) dot (p-a) <= |b-a|^2/2.

On this direction interval that excludes
phi_b<eta<phi_a+pi, so eta<=phi_b.

Similarly the unit edge into c, whose preceding vertex is not b and
is guarded, has direction zeta>=phi_b+pi. This is the same inequality
reversed, together with zeta-phi_c>=pi/3. Monotone convex path turning
then gives T_P>=zeta-eta>=pi, a contradiction. QED.

The lemma concerns a single site with wall contacts, not a coupled packet
with internal contacts or an arbitrary cell with deleted internal guards.
For a packet with all mutual distances strict and all internal path
vertices guarded, its individual wall contacts can be opened successively
by sufficiently small moves, while preserving every other finite strict
pair margin. Exact wall contacts in such an independent packet supply
no additional contact-budget mechanism. A general packet can instead
have a coupled internal contact graph; this argument does not release it.

## 10. Exact regressions and the remaining closure obligation

The companion rational verifier preserves three sharp checks.

1. At a right-angle hull tip, x=(t^2/4,t) opens its sole wall contact
   while entering the polygon. This checks the equality case in T.2.
2. For the unit path
   b,b+c,c,c+a,a, where
   a=(24/25,7/25), b=(-24/25,7/25), c=(0,-1), the turn is exactly pi.
   The inserted origin has three unit wall neighbors a,b,c, is strictly
   clear of the other wall vertices, and the full contact graph is
   triangle-free. Positive weights 25/14,25/14,1 strictly surround it.
   The origin is the only feasible site in its radius-1/2 neighborhood.
   Thus the strict half-turn scope in T.6 cannot be weakened to <=pi.
3. Take every integer point on [0,L] x {0}, L>=26, and hull isolates
   (1,+7),(1,-7). Its actual diameter is L, with sole pair (0,0),(L,0).
   Both convex outer cells share those loaded tips. After removing the
   common tip (0,0), the sites (1/20,+1/3),(1/20,-1/3) each strictly
   guard their unit wall and all old outside points, but their mutual
   squared distance is 4/9<1. This is an exact actual-diameter regression
   against gluing cells with shared loaded tips. Its force hull is a
   line and its degree-two core violates the canonical two-dimensional
   rigidity/normalization; it is not a macroscopic-cap counterexample.

The strained triangular cap, adaptive saturation, old diameter core,
nonexact cap antipodality, and retained isolates remain binding. No
proof-only closed-wall chart from Worker 1 has been substituted for Q.
The new endpoint gap (4) also rules out a continuous push of the old
loaded tips into their protected cell.

**Exact remaining paired-cap lemma:** force either the joint integer
population (10), or packets of total population m with complete local
triangle-freeness and paid ledger (9), or a same-cardinality global
strict-sub-D rearrangement. The finite source and cross-packet checks
are now proved under these exact hypotheses. The forced population
and any required motion of the rest of the core remain unproved.

This is not CAP-BRANCH CLOSED. It is a new finite loaded-endpoint and
paired-packet implication available for a future closing theorem.
